      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hi, i'm working on my sociology thesis and i'm getting a bit stuck on the interpretation. i ran a t-test on my survey data regarding social media usage and community engagement. my p-value came out as 0.042 and the 95% confidence interval for the difference in means is [0.12, 0.85]. could you explain how i should technically interpret these two specific values in my results section to show statistical significance?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A p-value of 0.042 indicates that the probability of observing the difference in means (or a more extreme difference) if the null hypothesis were true is approximately 4.2%. Since this probability is less than the typical significance level of 0.05, you can conclude that the difference is statistically significant. However, be cautious of the wording in your results section as the 95% confidence interval you provided is slightly below the accepted level of significance. You might want to phrase it like this, \"The observed difference in social media usage between the two groups was statistically significant (p = 0.042), occurring within the margin of our 95% confidence interval (95% CI: [0.12, 0.85]).\".",
